        {
          "subsection_id": "arterial_cannula_subsection_16",
          "subsection_title": "15. High-yield OSCE questions and answers",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "arterial_cannula_group_22",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q1. Identify the instrument."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "This is an arterial cannula, used for continuous invasive arterial blood pressure monitoring and repeated arterial blood sampling."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q2. What is the commonest insertion site?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The radial artery at the wrist."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q3. What is the main difference between an arterial cannula and a venous cannula?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The arterial cannula enters an artery for pressure monitoring and arterial sampling; a venous cannula provides venous access for appropriate fluids and medications."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q4. What are the major complications?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Bleeding, haematoma, infection, thrombosis, distal ischaemia, nerve injury and inaccurate pressure readings."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q5. What is the purpose of the pressure transducer?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It converts pressure changes transmitted through the fluid-filled tubing into electrical signals for display on the monitor."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q6. What does the dicrotic notch represent?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It is associated with aortic valve closure and the resulting pressure changes in the arterial waveform."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q7. Why must the transducer be levelled correctly?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Incorrect height introduces a hydrostatic pressure error and may lead to inaccurate blood pressure measurements."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q8. Can medications be routinely administered through an arterial cannula?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "No. It is intended for arterial monitoring and sampling, not routine drug administration. Accidental intra-arterial injection can cause severe tissue injury."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q9. What would you do if the patient's hand becomes cold and pale after radial cannulation?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Urgently assess distal perfusion and the cannulated limb, seek immediate clinical review and manage suspected arterial obstruction or ischaemia according to protocol."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
